# The ICS corpus is stored as the octets a CalDAV server would send: CRLF
# line endings, and tests that count them. Keep git out of the conversion.
test/harness/fixtures/ics/*.ics -text

# The crash corpus holds the inputs that the fuzzing lane found. An input
# carries the line endings that made it a finding, and one of them holds a
# carriage return that stands alone. A conversion of the line endings would
# take the finding out of the file.
test/harness/fixtures/ics-crash/*.ics -text

# The inputs of the golden gate carry CRLF line endings, and the golden files
# hold the octets that the canonical serializer writes. A conversion of the
# line endings would change the bytes under the test that compares them.
test/harness/fixtures/ics-serializer/**/*.ics -text

# The offset fixture states one answer for each line, and the reader of the
# fixture splits the file on the line feed.
test/harness/fixtures/timezone-offsets.txt -text

# The golden records hold the octets that the record writer writes, and a
# test compares them one octet at a time. The file uses the line feed
# alone, and a conversion of the line endings would change every line.
test/harness/fixtures/records/**/*.md -text
